# Error Handling Standards

## Core Error Handling Principles

### Comprehensive Error Coverage
- Every API call must handle both success and failure scenarios
- All async operations must include proper try-catch blocks
- Database operations must handle connection failures and data validation errors
- File operations must handle missing files, permission errors, and disk space issues

### User-Friendly Error Messages
- Never expose internal error details to end users
- Provide actionable feedback that helps users resolve issues
- Use consistent error message formatting across the application
- Implement proper internationalization for error messages

### Error Logging and Monitoring
- Log all errors with sufficient context for debugging
- Include stack traces for development environments only
- Implement structured logging with consistent formats
- Use error tracking services for production monitoring

## Implementation Patterns

### Frontend Error Handling
```typescript
// ✅ Proper error handling with user feedback
const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);
const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

const handleDataFetch = async () => {
  try {
    setIsLoading(true);
    setError(null);
    const data = await fetchUserData();
    // Handle success
  } catch (error) {
    const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'An unexpected error occurred';
    setError(message);
    toast.error('Failed to load data. Please try again.');
  } finally {
    setIsLoading(false);
  }
};
```

### API Route Error Handling
```typescript
// ✅ Comprehensive API error handling
export async function POST(request: Request) {
  try {
    const body = await request.json();
    
    // Validate input with Zod
    const validatedData = schema.parse(body);
    
    // Process request
    const result = await processData(validatedData);
    
    return NextResponse.json({ success: true, data: result });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof ZodError) {
      return NextResponse.json(
        { error: 'Invalid input data', details: error.errors },
        { status: 400 }
      );
    }
    
    if (error instanceof DatabaseError) {
      console.error('Database error:', error);
      return NextResponse.json(
        { error: 'Data operation failed. Please try again.' },
        { status: 500 }
      );
    }
    
    console.error('Unexpected error:', error);
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: 'An unexpected error occurred' },
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}
```

### Database Error Handling
- Always use transactions for multi-step operations
- Implement proper rollback mechanisms
- Handle constraint violations gracefully
- Validate data before database operations using Zod schemas

### File Processing Error Handling
- Validate file types and sizes before processing
- Handle corrupted or malformed files gracefully
- Implement timeout mechanisms for long-running operations
- Provide progress feedback for file uploads

## Error Categories and Responses

### Validation Errors (400)
- Input validation failures
- Schema validation errors
- Required field violations
- Format or type mismatches

### Authentication Errors (401)
- Invalid or expired tokens
- Missing authentication
- Insufficient permissions

### Authorization Errors (403)
- Access denied to resources
- Operation not permitted
- Role-based access violations

### Not Found Errors (404)
- Resource not found
- Invalid endpoints
- Deleted or moved content

### Server Errors (500)
- Database connection failures
- External service unavailable
- Unexpected application errors
- Configuration issues

## Error Recovery Strategies

### Automatic Retry
- Implement exponential backoff for transient failures
- Set maximum retry limits to prevent infinite loops
- Use circuit breaker patterns for external services
- Cache fallback data where appropriate

### Graceful Degradation
- Provide limited functionality when services are unavailable
- Use cached data when real-time data is inaccessible
- Disable non-critical features during partial outages
- Maintain core functionality even with component failures

### User Experience During Errors
- Show loading states during async operations
- Provide clear error messages with next steps
- Allow users to retry failed operations
- Maintain application state during error recovery

## Error Prevention

### Input Validation
- Use Zod schemas for all data validation
- Validate on both client and server sides
- Sanitize user inputs to prevent injection attacks
- Implement proper type checking throughout the application

### Defensive Programming
- Check for null/undefined values before operations
- Validate array bounds before accessing elements
- Use optional chaining and nullish coalescing
- Implement proper default values for missing data

### Type Safety
- Use strict TypeScript configuration in [tsconfig.json](mdc:frontend/tsconfig.json)
- Avoid any type usage
- Implement proper interface definitions
- Use discriminated unions for error states

## Monitoring and Alerting

### Error Tracking
- Implement error tracking service integration
- Monitor error rates and patterns
- Set up alerts for critical error thresholds
- Track user-impacting errors separately

### Performance Monitoring
- Monitor API response times
- Track database query performance
- Set up alerts for slow operations
- Monitor error rates by endpoint

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- ❌ Catching errors without handling them
- ❌ Exposing sensitive error information to users
- ❌ Using generic error messages that don't help users
- ❌ Ignoring error states in UI components
- ❌ Not logging sufficient context for debugging
- ❌ Letting errors crash the entire application
- ❌ Using string-based error handling instead of proper error types
- ❌ Not implementing proper cleanup in finally blocks
